= ECON 0100 | Exercise B1 | Demand

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  = Solutions

]

_Answers and work are shown in the red blocks._

Pumpkin pasties sell along the demand curve $P = 12 - Q/2$, in galleons and pasties.

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  *Plot.* Vertical intercept, $Q = 0$: $P = 12$. Horizontal intercept, $P = 0$: $0 = 12 - \frac{1}{2} Q \rightarrow Q = 24$. The line between them is demand; every answer below is a point on it or an area under it.
]

== Q1 | Quantity Demanded

a) What is the quantity demanded at 10 galleons? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $4$ pasties.

  Price to quantity: plug the price into the demand curve and solve for $Q$.

  #mitex(`
  10 = 12 - \tfrac{1}{2} Q \rightarrow \tfrac{1}{2} Q = 2 \rightarrow Q = 4
  `)
]

b) What is the marginal benefit at a quantity of 4? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $10$ galleons per pasty.

  Quantity to price: the height of the demand curve at $4$ pasties is the marginal benefit of the fourth pasty, what the buyer of that unit is willing to pay for it: $P = 12 - \frac{1}{2} \cdot 4 = 10$. Parts a) and b) are the same point $(4, 10)$ read in the two directions.
]

#pagebreak()

== Q2 | Consumer Surplus

Pumpkin pasties again, $P = 12 - Q/2$.

a) What is the quantity demanded at 5 galleons? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $14$ pasties.

  #mitex(`
  5 = 12 - \tfrac{1}{2} Q \rightarrow \tfrac{1}{2} Q = 7 \rightarrow Q = 14
  `)
]

b) Find and label the consumer surplus at that price.

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
    CS $= 49$ galleons.

      Consumer surplus is the triangle below demand and above the price, from $Q = 0$ out to the $14$ pasties bought. Its height is the intercept minus the price and its base is the quantity:

      #mitex(`
      \text{CS} = h \cdot b \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = (12 - 5) \cdot 14 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 49
      `)

      The rectangle below the price, $5 \cdot 14 = 70$, is what buyers pay, not surplus; the whole area under demand out to $14$, $119$, is total benefit. Surplus is benefit minus payment, $119 - 70 = 49$.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

